x86lint JIT dump hook for SpiderMonkey.

On x86-64 no SpiderMonkey spew channel prints encoding bytes: IONFLAGS=codegen
is AT&T assembly with offsets, and the shell's disnative() reaches one function
at a time from JS. This adds a writer to jit::Linker::newCode -- the one place
every JS JIT blob passes through, Ion, Baseline, RegExp and the trampolines
alike -- and a second call in wasm::CodeBlock::initialize, which every wasm
block reaches once its bytes are copied, linked and final: both compiler tiers
(recorded as WasmBaseline and WasmIon), a module's shared stubs and the lazy
entry stubs. The process-wide wasm builtin thunks are the one piece of
generated code not covered. Each process that links code appends its blobs to
the file named by X86LINT_JITDUMP plus ".<pid>", as the record
tools/jitdump2elf.py reads. Nothing happens unless the variable is set.

    cd /path/to/gecko
    git apply /path/to/x86lint/tools/spidermonkey-jitdump.patch
    ./mach build            # or make -C js/src in a JS-shell objdir

    X86LINT_JITDUMP=sm.jitdump dist/bin/js workload.js
    python3 /path/to/x86lint/tools/jitdump2elf.py sm.jitdump.<pid> -o sm.elf
    /path/to/x86lint/x86lint sm.elf

The pid suffix is what makes a browser dumpable: Firefox links JIT code in its
parent process and in every content process, and with one shared file name
they would truncate one another's output. A shell run leaves exactly one file.
For the browser, run headless with the content-process sandbox off
(MOZ_DISABLE_CONTENT_SANDBOX=1, or security.sandbox.content.level=0), since
the sandbox refuses the dump file's open(2); each process's file is then
converted on its own, which is also what keeps the parent's chrome JS apart
from the page's.

Verified against SpiderMonkey's own IONFLAGS=codegen listing: the dumped bytes
disassemble to the same instructions at the same offsets, with the patch
placeholders (movabsq $0x0) carrying their linked values, which is what a
linter should see.

